WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.622
Feedback is the mechanism that turns effort into improvement.

2
00:00:03.622 --> 00:00:09.256
Without feedback, the learner may repeat the same approach and mistake familiarity for progress.

3
00:00:09.256 --> 00:00:17.706
Useful feedback is timely enough to connect to the attempt, specific enough to guide correction, and grounded in examples or standards.

4
00:00:17.706 --> 00:00:23.340
It tells the learner what worked, what did not, and what to try differently.

5
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:31.388
Feedback can come from expert coaching, peer review, self-review, worked examples, rubrics, performance data, or comparison against strong examples.

6
00:00:31.388 --> 00:00:37.424
In technical work, a senior reviewer might explain why one hypothesis was stronger than another.

7
00:00:37.424 --> 00:00:42.656
In writing, a reviewer might point out where the main claim is buried.

8
00:00:42.656 --> 00:00:47.082
In leadership, a coach might identify where the conversation lost clarity.

9
00:00:47.082 --> 00:00:49.899
Correction is the next step after feedback.

10
00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:57.143
A comment or score is not enough if the learner does not adjust the method and try again.

11
00:00:57.143 --> 00:01:05.191
Correction may involve slowing down, using a checklist, practicing a smaller component, comparing two examples, or changing the decision rule.

12
00:01:05.191 --> 00:01:11.227
The value comes from closing the gap between the current performance and the target performance.

13
00:01:11.227 --> 00:01:14.849
Weak feedback environments create repeated mistakes and false confidence.

14
00:01:14.849 --> 00:01:24.909
If errors are hidden, delayed, or never connected back to the original action, learners can develop habits that feel normal but do not improve results.

15
00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:28.933
Deliberate practice makes errors visible while they are still correctable.

16
00:01:28.933 --> 00:01:36.982
That is uncomfortable at times, but it is much more useful than discovering the same gap during high-stakes work.
